\f0\b\fs68 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Article 2:\
How to Build the Loops That Just Replaced Entire Prompt Engineering \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 For two years, getting more out of an AI agent meant writing a better prompt.\cb1 \
\cb3 That era is quietly ending. The best engineers in the world stopped writing prompts a while ago and started writing loops - systems that prompt the agent, check the result, and keep running until the work is done.\cb1 \
\cb3 Karpathy's overnight script found 20 optimizations he had missed in two decades of tuning by hand. Boris Cherny, who built Claude Code, has not written a line of code this year. Anthropic engineers now merge close to 8x more code per day than they did in 2024.\cb1 \
\cb3 None of them typed anything smarter. They stopped typing at all.\cb1 \
\cb3 Here is what a loop actually is, when you need one, when you do not, and how to build your first one.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs30 \cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f6\b\fs52 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 700 experiments while the human slept\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 March 2026. Andrej Karpathy pushes three files to GitHub. Around 630 lines of code.\cb1 \
\cb3 One file held the model. One scored it. One told the agent what to explore and what to leave alone. The agent could only touch the training file. Nothing else.\cb1 \
\cb3 The cycle was boring on purpose. Read the code, propose a change, train for five minutes, check if the score improved, keep the change if it did, roll back if it did not, then go again.\cb1 \
\cb3 He pointed it at a model he had already tuned by hand for two decades. He let it run for two days.\cb1 \
\cb3 It ran 700 experiments. It found 20 improvements. One of them was a missing scalar multiplier in the attention mechanism, subtle, not the kind of bug any linter would ever catch, but exactly the kind of thing a careful engineer could have found and never did.\cb1 \
\cb3 Shopify's CEO ran the same trick overnight on an internal model. He woke up to a 19 percent quality gain on a model half the size of the previous one.\cb1 \
\cb3 The insight is not "the AI is smarter." Humans get tired after experiment twelve. Loops do not get tired at all.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/hanakoxbt/article/2077387678241141070/media/2077378563238481920"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs30 \cf4 \strokec4 \
\
}}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f6\b\fs52 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 A loop is not a prompt on a schedule\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 Most people hear "loop" and think cron. That misses the whole thing.\cb1 \
\cb3 A prompt is one instruction. A loop is a goal the AI keeps working toward until it gets there, with nobody in the chair. It plans, executes, verifies its own result, feeds the result back in, and repeats.\cb1 \
\cb3 Five stages: discover, plan, execute, verify, iterate. Three of them do the actual work.\cb1 \
\cb3 The verifier is the heart of the loop. Without a real gate on the output, you do not have a loop, you have the agent grading its own homework forever. A gate is a test that passes or fails, a build that compiles or crashes, a linter that returns zero or non-zero. Not a second agent with an opinion.\cb1 \
\cb3 State is what makes the loop learn. A file on the side, a Linear board, a project log, somewhere outside the conversation that records what was tried and what failed. Tomorrow's run resumes instead of starting from scratch.\cb1 \
\cb3 A stop condition is what keeps it sane. Every real loop has two exits: the goal is met, or a hard cap fires. Skip this and you have built a machine that runs all night for nothing.\cb1 \
\cb3 Miss any of the three and you have not built a loop. You have built an expensive script.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs30 \cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f6\b\fs52 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 The four conditions before you build one\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 Loops earn their cost only when four things are true at the same time. Miss one and the setup takes more than it returns.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf4 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 The task repeats at least weekly. Less than that and the setup cost never amortizes.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Something can automatically fail the work. A test, a type check, a linter, a build.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Your token budget can absorb the waste. Loops re-read context, retry, explore.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 The agent has senior engineer tools. Logs, a reproduction environment, the ability to run the code it writes and see what breaks.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 The honest version, the part nobody selling loops will tell you: most people do not need the heavy version yet. If you are on a consumer plan trying to run overnight verification loops on serious work, the token bill arrives before the productivity gain does.\cb1 \
\cb3 Good first loops are the boring ones. CI triage. Dependency bumps. Lint-and-fix passes. Flaky test reproduction. Issue-to-PR drafts on a codebase with strong tests already in place.\cb1 \
\cb3 Bad first loops are the interesting ones. Architecture rewrites. Auth code. Payments. Anything where "done" is a judgment call and a human still has to weigh in.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs30 \cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f6\b\fs52 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 The five blocks that make a loop real\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 Every real loop is assembled from the same five pieces. Claude Code and Codex ship all of them now.\cb1 \
\cb3 The first is automation. The heartbeat. Something that fires the loop on a schedule or an event. /loop runs on a cadence. /goal keeps going until a condition you defined actually holds. Without a heartbeat, the loop is a script you ran once and forgot about.\cb1 \
\cb3 The second is a skill. Project knowledge saved as a claude-md file the agent reads on every run. Without it, the loop re-derives your context from zero each cycle. With it, intent compounds. The loop knows your conventions, your build steps, the thing you never do because of that one incident three months ago.\cb1 \
\cb3 The third is sub-agents. The maker and the checker cannot be the same model. The one that wrote the code is far too generous grading its own work. The one that wrote the article misses its own weak sections. Writer fast and cheap. Reviewer slow and strict. That separation is most of the quality.\cb1 \
\cb3 The fourth is connectors. The loop opens the pull request, closes the ticket, pings the channel when CI turns green. This is the difference between an agent that says "here is a suggested fix" and a report waiting for you in the morning saying the PR is already merged.\cb1 \
\cb3 The fifth is the verifier. The test, type check, or build that fails bad work automatically. Everything else is plumbing. This is the block that decides whether the loop helps you or just spends your money.\cb1 \
\cb3 Stack these together and you get what serious teams now run at scale. Dozens of loops, each owning one narrow job, running side by side while everyone sleeps. One engineer used a fleet loop like this to rewrite an entire codebase from one language to another in about six days, work that would have taken close to a year by hand.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
